# Document Root and Element Inflation [SPEC:LTS-DOC-ROOT]

> Five documents in this corpus declare their own identity on a bare line after the h1, and for all five
> that identity DOES NOT EXIST as a node. Their `domain`, `status`, `premise` and `thesis` attach to
> nothing; a prefix query cannot enumerate them; their children carry `parent_section: null` and no
> CONTAINS edge, so "open this spec and show me its elements" returns nothing. The five are EXACTLY the
> five files with no frontmatter — identical sets — which is what settled the remedy: this is one
> authoring lapse with two symptoms, not a second convention in use. David ruled on 2026-08-11 that the
> five are REPAIRED to the established pattern and the parser is NOT taught a second spelling. So this
> spec makes the document root form a REQUIRED, LINTED contract (a repair alone lets file 73 repeat the
> lapse), then keeps the three parts that never depended on where the root lives. Every document MUST
> declare its own element namespace in frontmatter — mandatory, because as an opt-in the check below
> fires zero times until somebody volunteers, and a rule that reports zero looks exactly like a rule that
> works. That declaration is a checksum over structurally-derived containment, never the containment
> mechanism, and it states what the author AUTHORED as an element — including ids that currently fail to
> mint, because those failures are what the instrument is for. On top of it the INFLATION CHECK reports a
> bracket authored AS an element that fell through to prose. Last, the bracketed-key silent-loss class is
> named, with the general rule it proves — a control that does not exercise the shape found in live
> corpora certifies a narrower class than its name claims.

---

## 0. What is broken, measured

### [DRI-THESIS] A document that cannot address itself cannot be queried, traversed, or rendered as a unit

- premise: a `.lts` document is a container that serializes a graph fragment. If the container itself is
  not a node, every question of the form "what is in this document" degrades from a traversal into a
  filename guess, and every property the author wrote ABOUT the document attaches to nothing.
- requirement: a document MUST have exactly one root node, and that root MUST be reachable by the same
  mechanisms as any other node — `lts_find(prefix=...)`, `lts_node(id)`, and a CONTAINS traversal to its
  children.
- key-insight: the failure is not a missing FEATURE. Heading-nesting containment, structural CONTAINS
  edges and `parent_section` all work today and are verified working in the 67 conforming files. The five
  broken files fail at ONE point — the line the author wrote their id on is not a heading, so it mints
  nothing — and every downstream absence follows mechanically from that single point.
- consequence: the remedy is therefore an AUTHORING repair plus a rule that prevents recurrence, not a
  parser change ([DRI-REPAIR] records why, with the measurement that decided it).
- criticality: high
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-DOC-ROOT]

### [DRI-MEASURED] The census: the bare-line set and the no-frontmatter set are IDENTICAL

- census: all 72 `.lts` files under `corpora/` were classified by where the document id sits and by
  whether they carry frontmatter.

| Form | Count | Result today |
|------|-------|--------------|
| id in the h1 — `# Title [TAG:ID]`, with frontmatter | **67** | root mints; children carry `parent_section` + structural CONTAINS |
| id on a BARE LINE after a bracketless h1, and NO frontmatter | **5** | **no root node exists**; ~20 property lines orphaned per file; children `parent_section: null` |
| neither an h1 id nor a bare-line declaration | **0** | — |

- the decisive measurement: the five files with a bare-line root are EXACTLY the five files with no
  frontmatter. Identical sets, no overlap error, no third case. That is what establishes this as ONE
  authoring lapse with two symptoms rather than a second convention in use, and it is the evidence
  David's ruling rests on ([DRI-REPAIR]).
- the five: `design-excellence/design-excellence-composeplatform.lts`,
  `insights-and-warts/insights_and_warts.lts`, `platform-specs/40-decisions-tab.lts`,
  `platform-specs/41-intelligence-host-spawner.lts`, `platform-specs/42-idle-tracking.lts`. Their
  declared-but-unminted ids are `SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-HOST`, `SPEC:CXC-DECISIONS-TAB`,
  `SPEC:CXC-IDLE-TRACKING`, `DOC-DX`, `LEDGER:INSIGHTS-WARTS` (written UNBRACKETED on purpose —
  bracketing an id that by construction does not exist would mint a dangling reference from this spec).
- second population: 4 further files carry frontmatter but no `audience` key, which is a standing
  ratified requirement. **5 + 4 = 9 files fail the frontmatter/h1 half of the required form**, and that 9
  is the exact positive control for that half's first run ([DRI-ACCEPT], DRI-C1).
- third population, and it is the whole corpus: **0 of 72 files declare `elements` today — including the
  exemplar.** That is the exact count the newly-required key must report on its first run: 72 findings,
  one per document, and any other number means the rule is not reading every file ([DRI-ACCEPT], DRI-C1B).
  A baseline of zero is not an embarrassment; it is what makes the first run a real measurement instead
  of a confirmation.
- coverability, measured (this is what makes the requirement feasible rather than aspirational): **67 of
  71 files are coverable by at most THREE element patterns.** Requiring a narrowest-covering declaration
  is therefore a one-line-per-file act for the overwhelming majority. The four files that are NOT
  coverable in three are the interesting ones — a document whose ids do not fall into three families is
  either carrying several documents' worth of subject matter or naming its nodes ad hoc, and the
  declaration is what surfaces that. Those four MUST be named by the first run rather than exempted
  ([DRI-ELEMENTS-NARROW]).
- corroboration, independently derived: the `.lts` domain profile's own
  [GUIDANCE-UNTAGGED-H1] note names the same five files and states the same consequence — "they mint no
  document node and no `document_tag` ... invisible to any query addressing a document by tag" — from a
  separate corpus scan. Two derivations, one number.
- **D1 — the root does not exist.** Verified by direct query: `CXC-INTELLIGENCE-HOST`,
  `CXC-DECISIONS-TAB` and `CXC-IDLE-TRACKING` return no node. Cause: the parser mints ids only from
  BRACKETED HEADINGS, so a column-0 bracketed line in a body is read as prose.
- **D2 — a bracketed id in the PROPERTY KEY position mints nothing and warns nothing.** `CXC-IH-EDGES`
  in spec 41 is titled "Decompositional edges this realizes" and its entire body is three lines of the
  shape shown at [DRI-BRACKET-KEY]. Live result: `properties: {}`, `edges: {}`, `edge_total: 0`, and
  `lts_lint` reports ZERO findings of any kind on those lines. A node that exists to declare three edges
  declares none, silently. Cause is two anchors agreeing: `_PROP_SIMPLE_RE` requires a lowercase key, and
  the coverage near-miss classifier's `_KEY_COLON_RE` also anchors on a letter, so the line is not even a
  near-miss. Measured: 3 occurrences, all in spec 41.
- **D3 — the SL-1 control is narrower than the class it claims.** `lts_selfcheck` passes
  `CASE-SL1-SYMBOL` ("a symbol-prefixed key does not parse, and IS flagged") — but that control authors
  an emoji-prefixed key. Every other bracketed line in the control file is either a P4 nested VALUE or a
  Relationships triple. NONE is `- [ID]: description`. The control never exercises D2's shape, and a
  green selfcheck reads as "SL-1 is handled".
- provenance: David and the composeux-architect, 2026-08-11, verified against the live engine (query,
  lint and selfcheck), not inferred from the grammar.
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-DOC-ROOT]

---

## 1. The required document root form

### [DRI-ROOT-REQUIRED] Frontmatter with `audience` and `elements`, and the id IN THE H1 — a linted contract, not a convention

- requirement: every `.lts` document MUST open with a YAML frontmatter block, and that block MUST include
  `audience` (a standing ratified rule this node makes MECHANICALLY ENFORCED rather than remembered) and
  `elements` (ruled mandatory by David 2026-08-11; the rule and its reasoning are at
  [DRI-ELEMENTS-DECL]).
- requirement: every `.lts` document MUST declare its id in its `h1`, in the form
  `# Title [TAG:ID]`. The id MUST NOT be declared on any other line.
- requirement: a file failing either rule MUST produce a lint finding, and that finding MUST name BOTH
  the rule it broke AND the conforming exemplar — `corpora/platform-specs/29-composeux-embedded-reactive.lts`
  — so the fix is obvious from the message alone without opening a spec.
- rationale (why the exemplar is in the message, not just the rule): a finding that states a rule leaves
  the author to reconstruct the shape; a finding that names a file the author can open and copy removes
  the reconstruction entirely. The corpus already pays for findings nobody acts on, and the difference is
  almost always whether the remedy was in the message.
- exemplar, stated so the rule has one referent and not a paraphrase: spec 29 carries `scope`,
  `audience`, `boundary`, `not-this`, `depends-on`, `domain`, `confidentiality`, `artifact-readiness` in
  frontmatter; then `# ComposeUX Reactive Embedded Tier [SPEC:CXC-EMBED-REACTIVE]`; then a blockquote
  summary; then element headings each carrying `- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-EMBED-REACTIVE]`. It is the exemplar
  for everything EXCEPT `elements`, which no file carries yet — spec 29 needs `elements: [CXC-ER-*]`
  added like every other file, and saying so here keeps the exemplar honest rather than exempt.
- default (STATED): the check runs on EVERY `.lts` in the corpus with no opt-out and no per-file
  suppression. A structural contract with an escape hatch is a convention again, which is the state this
  node exists to leave.
- severity: `audience` missing is an ERROR (a ratified requirement with 4 live violations, a bounded and
  closable set). `elements` missing is an ERROR (72 live violations on day one — see the migration note
  below). A missing frontmatter BLOCK is an ERROR. A bracketless h1 is an ERROR.
- migration note (stated so the first run is not mistaken for a broken linter): the `elements` rule
  begins at 72 of 72 failing, because the key is new. That is expected and it is the point — the count is
  a worklist with an exact size, and it goes to zero by adding one line per file. It MUST NOT be softened
  to a warning to make the first run look better; a warning at 72 is a warning nobody reads, and the
  denominator argument at [DRI-ELEMENTS-DECL] is precisely about not letting this check report a
  comfortable number.
- error-case: an `h1` carrying a bracketed id that fails the id grammar (lowercase, punctuation, a
  leading digit) MUST be reported as a bracketless h1 with the failed condition named. It MUST NOT be
  normalized and accepted — the uppercase requirement is the mechanical signal that separates an id from
  ordinary bracketed prose.
- error-case: a file with MORE than one `h1`, or with a bracketed id on a second `h1`, MUST be an error
  naming both lines. One document, one root; picking a winner silently makes a reference to the loser
  permanently dangling with no finding.
- error-case: a bracketed id appearing at column 0 on a non-heading line anywhere in the first 10 lines
  of a file whose h1 is bracketless MUST be named in the finding as the probable intended root, so the
  message says "you wrote it here, it belongs in the h1" rather than only "your h1 has no id". This is
  the one place the bare-line form is acknowledged: as a diagnostic hint, never as a parse.
- scenario:
  - when: the check is run over the corpus for the first time
  - then: exactly 9 files are named for the frontmatter/h1 half — the 5 with no frontmatter and no h1 id,
    and the 4 with frontmatter lacking `audience` — and separately all 72 are named for the missing
    `elements` key; every finding names spec 29
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-DOC-ROOT]

### [DRI-ROOT-BY-DOCTYPE] The h1 id form for EVERY document type, not just `[SPEC:…]`

- requirement: the rule is expressed over the document tag, and the tag MUST be one the domain profile or
  FactLattice core already registers. A repair MUST NOT invent a tag to fit a file.
- registered tags and their h1 form:

| Tag | Source | h1 form | Live example |
|-----|--------|---------|--------------|
| `SPEC` | core | `# Title [SPEC:ID]` | `# ComposeUX Reactive Embedded Tier [SPEC:CXC-EMBED-REACTIVE]` |
| `SYSTEM` | core | `# Title [SYSTEM:ID]` | 1 document |
| `PROJECT` | core | `# Title [PROJECT:ID]` | 1 document |
| `LEDGER` | domain profile | `# Title [LEDGER:ID]` | `# ... [LEDGER:PRINCIPLES]`, `[LEDGER:STRATEGIES]`, `[LEDGER:DELIV-LEDGER]` |
| `REGISTRY` | domain profile | `# Title [REGISTRY:ID]` | `[REGISTRY:PROJECTS]` |
| `PROFILE` | domain profile | `# Title [PROFILE:ID]` | `[PROFILE:PROFILE-COMPOSEIO]` |

- requirement: the two non-SPEC documents among the five are repaired to REGISTERED tags, and their ids
  are preserved unchanged ([DRI-NO-MIGRATION]):
  - `insights_and_warts.lts` already declares the tag `LEDGER` on its bare line; the repair moves it into
    the h1 as `[LEDGER:INSIGHTS-WARTS]` and changes nothing else about the identity.
  - `design-excellence-composeplatform.lts` declares `DOC-DX` with NO tag at all. `DOC` is not a
    registered document tag; `DOC-DX` is an id whose type prefix is `DOC`. The repair takes the tag from
    the nearest sibling precedent — `principles.lts` is `[LEDGER:PRINCIPLES]`, and a design-excellence
    principles corpus is the same shape — giving `[LEDGER:DOC-DX]`.
- note (small, deliberately NOT a decision node): registering a `DOC` tag in the profile is the
  alternative to reusing `LEDGER`, and it is one line in
  `corpora/intelligence-console/platform-composeio-profile.lts`. It is left as a profile-registration
  question rather than a design fork because under EITHER answer the document ends with a registered tag
  in its h1 and no doctype-filtered query silently omits it — which was the only force the question ever
  had. Whoever performs the repair takes `LEDGER` unless the profile has gained `DOC` by then.
- requirement: a document whose h1 carries a bracketed id with NO tag (`# Title [ID]`) MUST be reported.
  An untagged root is invisible to every `find(doctype=…)`, and a query that silently omits one document
  is the same defect shape this spec removes.
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-DOC-ROOT]

### [DRI-REPAIR] Repair the five files to the exemplar — and the RECORDED REJECTION of the parser-side alternative

- requirement: each of the five files is repaired by (a) adding a frontmatter block carrying at minimum
  `scope`, `audience`, `elements`, `boundary`, `not-this`, `domain`, `confidentiality`,
  `artifact-readiness`, per the exemplar plus the newly-required `elements` key; (b) moving the bracketed
  id from the bare line INTO the h1; (c) leaving the `- key: value` lines exactly where they are,
  directly beneath the h1, where they now attach to the newly-minted root instead of to nothing.
- requirement: the bare line's trailing description becomes the document's blockquote summary line
  immediately after the h1, matching the exemplar. It MUST NOT be discarded — it is the one-sentence
  statement of what the document is, and it is the only place several of these documents have one.
- requirement: the repair MUST NOT rename any node id, in the h1 or below it ([DRI-NO-MIGRATION]).
- the five, with their repaired h1:

| File | Repaired h1 id | Also needs |
|------|----------------|-----------|
| `40-decisions-tab.lts` | `[SPEC:CXC-DECISIONS-TAB]` | frontmatter incl. `audience` |
| `41-intelligence-host-spawner.lts` | `[SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-HOST]` | frontmatter incl. `audience`; also carries the 3 bracketed-key lines ([DRI-BRACKET-KEY]) |
| `42-idle-tracking.lts` | `[SPEC:CXC-IDLE-TRACKING]` | frontmatter incl. `audience` |
| `insights_and_warts.lts` | `[LEDGER:INSIGHTS-WARTS]` | frontmatter incl. `audience` |
| `design-excellence-composeplatform.lts` | `[LEDGER:DOC-DX]` | frontmatter incl. `audience`; tag chosen per [DRI-ROOT-BY-DOCTYPE] |

- change: "REJECTED — teaching the parser to mint a document root from a bare `[TAG:ID]` line following a
  bracketless h1. Ruled by David 2026-08-11. The five files are REPAIRED to the established pattern
  instead, and the pattern is made a linted contract ([DRI-ROOT-REQUIRED])."
- context_for_change:
    prior_state: "This spec's first draft specified the bare-line root as a parser rule (a
      root-declaration line recognised on the first non-blank line after a bracketless h1, binding
      properties, content span and nesting), on the reasoning that it routed an existing, working
      containment mechanism to five files already authored in that form and required no edit to the
      conforming majority."
    reasoning: "The census removed the premise. The five bare-line files are EXACTLY the five files with
      no frontmatter — identical sets — so the bare-line form is not a second convention in use, it is
      one authoring lapse with two symptoms. Those files are simultaneously non-conformant on a standing
      ratified rule (`audience` is REQUIRED frontmatter), so they must be edited regardless; the parser
      change would therefore have bought nothing and cost a second spelling of one construct."
    rejected_alternative: "Recognise the bare-line root in the parser and leave the five files as
      authored. Its real strength was that it needed no edit to any file and introduced no new semantics
      — every clause was an existing rule applied to a newly-minted node. That strength evaporates once
      the files must be edited anyway."
    counter_argument: "P2 Notation Freedom exists precisely so a parser can accept more than one surface
      form for the same abstract construct, and this would have been one more accepted form."
    counter_response: "Notation Freedom is for ENCODING flexibility — the same knowledge expressed in a
      different notation (a YAML block, a table, a list) — not for two spellings of one construct. Two
      spellings is the two-conventions problem this corpus spent the day removing: every consumer must
      then handle both, every author must choose, and the choice carries no meaning. The cost is
      permanent and the benefit was five files."
    invalidating_assumption: "That the bare-line form is confined to an already-non-conformant set that
      must be edited for other reasons. If a bare-line root ever appears in a file that is otherwise
      fully conformant — frontmatter complete, `audience` present — then it IS a second convention in
      genuine use, the lapse theory is falsified, and the parser-side answer must be reconsidered on its
      merits. Also invalidated if the population grows large enough that repair cost exceeds the cost of
      the second spelling, though at 5 of 72 it is nowhere near."
    implication: "R1 leaves this spec entirely. [DD-DRI-ROOT-TITLE] and [DD-DRI-UNTAGGED-ROOT] dissolve
      with it — with the id in the h1 there is one title rule and one existing form — and the untagged-id
      question survives only as the small note at [DRI-ROOT-BY-DOCTYPE]. R2, R3 and R4 are untouched:
      none of them ever depended on where the root lives."
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-DOC-ROOT]

### [DRI-NO-MIGRATION] Zero changes to the 67 conforming files, and zero changes to any node id

- requirement (LOAD-BEARING for adoption): the repair and the new lint rules MUST NOT require an edit to
  any of the 67 files that already carry frontmatter and mint a root from their h1.
- requirement: no node id may be renamed, re-prefixed or re-namespaced by this work — not a document
  root, not an element ([DRI-ID-GRAMMAR] states why the tempting migration is actively wrong).
- testability: this is checkable as a whole-corpus diff and it is the cheapest gate in the spec — parse
  the corpus before and after, assert the node set for the 67 conforming files is byte-identical and that
  exactly five roots plus their structural edges appeared.
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-DOC-ROOT]

---

## 2. The element declaration — MANDATORY, narrowest, and about intent

### [DRI-ELEMENTS-DECL] Every document MUST declare its element namespace in frontmatter — mandatory, not optional

- definition: `elements` is a REQUIRED frontmatter key whose value is one or more element-prefix
  PATTERNS. It declares what this document claims to contain. It has NO effect on the graph.
- requirement: every `.lts` document MUST carry `elements` in frontmatter. A document that does not is a
  lint finding at ERROR severity, exactly as a missing `audience` is ([DRI-ROOT-REQUIRED]).
- rationale (this is why mandatory, and it is the argument this corpus keeps re-earning): as an OPT-IN,
  the inflation check ([DRI-INFLATION]) fires ZERO times until somebody volunteers a declaration — and a
  rule that reports zero looks exactly like a rule that works. There is no way, from the outside, to tell
  a clean corpus from an unrun check. Mandatory is what gives the check a DENOMINATOR: every document is
  in scope, so a zero is a measurement rather than an absence of measurement.
- requirement (the whole point, and a builder MUST NOT weaken it): containment remains STRUCTURALLY
  DERIVED from heading nesting. The declaration MUST NOT create, suppress, re-parent or filter a single
  edge. There is exactly one source of truth for the graph, and this is a checksum over it — the same
  relationship a control digest has to its control file, and it is worth exactly as much for the same
  reason: a digest that can change the artifact it digests certifies nothing.
- grammar: a pattern is `PREFIX*` — an id-grammar prefix followed by a TRAILING wildcard only. In
  frontmatter the canonical form is a YAML list, `elements: [CXC-ER-*]` or
  `elements: [DRI-*, DD-DRI-*]`; a single bare pattern is accepted for the common one-family case. A
  mid-pattern wildcard MUST be refused at lint time.
- rationale (trailing-glob only): it is the same restriction the platform's subscription pattern language
  already carries, for the same reason — a trailing glob over a hyphen-structured name is a prefix test,
  which is total, order-free and cheap, while a mid-pattern glob demands a matcher and admits patterns
  whose intended meaning two readers will not agree on. Reusing a decided rule prevents a second pattern
  language in one corpus.
- rationale (why frontmatter, not a root property): frontmatter is where document-level metadata already
  lives and is readable WITHOUT parsing the body, which is exactly the property the missing-key check
  needs — the linter must be able to say "this document declares nothing" about a document it may not be
  able to parse fully. It also puts `elements` beside `audience`, so one glance at a frontmatter block
  answers whether a file conforms.
- requirement: the ROOT's own id is exempt from the misfiled check ([DRI-CONVERSE]). A root id names the
  document and is routinely outside its own element family — `CXC-INTELLIGENCE-HOST` is not in
  `CXC-IH-*` — and a rule that forced it inside would either rename five roots or produce five permanent
  findings.
- worked example (shown indented so this document's own parse is not perturbed; spec 41 after repair):

        ---
        scope: "..."
        audience: "..."
        elements: [CXC-IH-*]
        domain: plt.cux
        ---
        # IntelligenceHost — the console spawns carried-identity peer intelligences [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-HOST]

        > The console-owned harness that instantiates N parallel intelligences.

- dogfooding: this document carries `elements: [DRI-*, DD-DRI-*]` in its own frontmatter. The declaration
  is inert until the check ships; it is authored now so the first implementation has a live two-pattern
  example that is not one of its own fixtures.
- see-also: [DRI-ELEMENTS-NARROW], [DRI-ELEMENTS-INTENT], [DRI-INFLATION]
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-DOC-ROOT]

### [DRI-ELEMENTS-NARROW] The pattern MUST be the NARROWEST that covers — a namespace nobody owns is not a namespace

- requirement: the declared pattern MUST be the narrowest prefix that covers the document's elements. A
  broader prefix that happens to cover is NOT acceptable merely because it matches.
- measured, and it is why this rule exists: `CXC-*` covers 100% of spec 29's elements and is WORTHLESS,
  because nearly every spec in the corpus uses `CXC-`. The useful declaration is `CXC-ER-*`. A pattern
  that covers everything discriminates nothing: under `CXC-*` the misfiled check can never fire, the leak
  check can never fire, and the inflation check's candidate set becomes the whole corpus's vocabulary. A
  declaration that cannot fail is not an assertion.
- requirement: a pattern declared by MORE THAN ONE document is itself a finding, naming every document
  that declares it. A namespace nobody owns is not a namespace — it is a prefix — and the shared case
  means either the family should be split or the documents should be merged. (This is the same condition
  as [DRI-CONVERSE]'s SHARED-FAMILY warning, raised here to a rule about the DECLARATION rather than an
  observation about the nodes.)
- feasibility, measured: **67 of 71 files are coverable by at most THREE patterns**, so the narrowness
  requirement is a one-line act for the overwhelming majority and does not force anyone to enumerate
  their nodes. The requirement is bounded by that measurement, not by optimism.
- requirement: the FOUR files not coverable in three patterns MUST be named by the first run and MUST NOT
  be exempted. A document whose ids do not fall into a small number of families is either carrying
  several documents' worth of subject matter or naming its nodes ad hoc; both are worth knowing and
  neither is fixed by widening the pattern.
- default (STATED): the ceiling is THREE patterns per document. Above three, the file is reported as
  over-fragmented rather than refused — the declaration still stands and the checks still run, but the
  count is surfaced so the underlying condition is visible instead of absorbed.
- error-case: a declared pattern that is a strict prefix of another declared pattern in the SAME document
  (`CXC-*` alongside `CXC-ER-*`) MUST be refused. The broader one makes the narrower one dead text and
  re-introduces exactly the worthless-coverage case this node forbids.
- scenario:
  - when: spec 29 declares `elements: [CXC-*]`
  - then: the linter refuses it, names `CXC-ER-*` as the narrowest pattern covering the document's actual
    element ids, and states that `CXC-*` is declared by many documents
- see-also: [DRI-CONVERSE]
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-DOC-ROOT]

### [DRI-ELEMENTS-INTENT] ⛔ The declaration expresses AUTHORED INTENT, not current output — do NOT derive it from what a file mints

- requirement (LOAD-BEARING; the single most likely wrong implementation of this whole spec): `elements`
  covers every id the document AUTHORS as an element, INCLUDING ids that currently fail to inflate. It
  MUST NOT be derived, generated, or auto-populated from the set of nodes a file currently MINTS.
- rationale: a declaration derived from current output is a control fitted to its own result. Every
  existing file would pass by construction, the inflation check would report zero on the corpus it was
  written for, and the zero would be indistinguishable from success. That is precisely the failure D3
  already documents one layer down — the SL-1 control that passes because it exercises a shape the live
  corpus does not contain — reproduced at the level of the whole check rather than one case.
- worked consequence, stated because the natural implementation is the wrong one: spec 41 contains
  `[CXC-IH:5A]`, which never minted. Under this rule that file still declares `CXC-IH-*`, so the
  inflation check FAILS on it. **That failure is the instrument working.** An implementation that
  produced a declaration under which spec 41 passes has removed the only evidence the check was ever
  going to find.
- requirement: any tooling that OFFERS a declaration (a scaffold, a fixer, an agent) MUST present it as a
  starting suggestion derived from minted nodes AND state, in the same breath, that the author must widen
  it to cover ids they intended but which did not mint. It MUST NOT write the derived value silently.
- error-case: a corpus-wide run in which the inflation check reports ZERO findings against declarations
  that were machine-derived MUST be treated as UNVALIDATED, not as clean. The correct response is to
  check whether any declaration was derived, not to publish the zero.
- testability: this is checkable directly — take a file with a known non-minting authored id, derive a
  declaration from its minted nodes, and assert the derived declaration does NOT cover the known id.
  That assertion failing is what a fitted control looks like from the inside.
- criticality: high
- see-also: [DRI-CONTROL-SHAPE]
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-DOC-ROOT]

---

## 3. The inflation check and its three converses

### [DRI-INFLATION] A bracket that matches a declared element prefix and produced NO NODE is an INFLATION FAILURE

- definition: INFLATION is the gap between what a document declared it would contain and what it actually
  minted. An inflation failure is a bracket in the file whose text matches a declared element prefix but
  for which no node exists — a thing authored AS an element that fell through to prose.
- requirement: for every document with a declared `elements` set, the linter MUST report every inflation
  failure, naming the file, the line, the bracket text as written, and the normalized form that matched
  the declared prefix.
- rationale (this is the sharp part, and it is why the check is worth building): every other check in
  this spec catches an OUTCOME — a node that is missing, a declaration that is empty. This one catches
  INTENT. A mis-punctuated `[CXC-IH:5A]`, a lowercase `[cxc-ih-5a]`, a typo'd `[CXC-IH-5AA]` — today all
  three are indistinguishable from a sentence, and the corpus has no mechanism that can tell an author
  they wrote a node and got prose. Declaring the namespace is what makes the author's intent
  machine-readable at all.
- requirement: resolution is CORPUS-scoped, not file-scoped. A bracket is an inflation failure only when
  its raw text is the id of NO node anywhere in the corpus. A bracket that resolves to a real node in
  another file is NOT inflation; it is a reference, and if it matches this file's declared prefix it is a
  namespace leak instead ([DRI-CONVERSE]). Getting this scope wrong in either direction is the difference
  between a useful check and one that fires on every cross-file reference.
- severity: ERROR. The declaration is MANDATORY ([DRI-ELEMENTS-DECL]), so every document is held to one
  and the check has a denominator; downgrading the answer to a warning would put the finding in the same
  bucket as the backlog it is meant to be read against.
- requirement: because the declaration is mandatory, the inflation check runs on EVERY document. Its
  finding count on the corpus is therefore a real measurement of the corpus's element hygiene rather than
  a measurement of how many authors opted in — which is the whole reason the declaration was made
  mandatory rather than offered.
- error-case: a bracket that matches a declared prefix, mints no node, AND whose normalized form is a
  valid id that also does not exist anywhere — that is the common typo, and the finding MUST say so
  rather than emitting two overlapping findings for one line.
- scenario:
  - when: spec 41 declares `elements: CXC-IH-*` and a body line references `[CXC-IH:5A]`
  - then: the linter reports an inflation failure at that line, states that `CXC-IH:5A` normalizes to
    `CXC-IH-5A` which matches the declared prefix, and that no such node exists — today this line
    produces nothing at all
- see-also: [DRI-INFLATION-MATCH], [DRI-INFLATION-SCOPE]
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-DOC-ROOT]

### [DRI-INFLATION-MATCH] The normalization rule — the check must see through the punctuation that broke the id

- requirement: candidacy is decided on a NORMALIZED form of the bracket text, because the failures worth
  catching are precisely those where the punctuation is what broke it. Raw prefix comparison would miss
  `[CXC-IH:5A]` entirely, since `CXC-IH:5A` does not start with `CXC-IH-`, and missing that case would
  leave the check unable to catch the exact example that motivated it.
- normalization (total, deterministic, applied in this order):

| # | Step |
|---|------|
| N1 | take the raw characters between `[` and the first `]` |
| N2 | uppercase |
| N3 | replace every run of characters outside `[A-Z0-9]` with a single `-` |
| N4 | strip leading and trailing `-` |

- requirement: a declared pattern `PREFIX*` matches when the normalized text starts with the normalized
  PREFIX. The comparison is a prefix test and nothing more — no edit distance, no fuzzy match, no token
  reordering. A heuristic that guesses at similarity is how a widened matcher manufactures findings, and
  the corpus has already paid for one widened matcher fabricating edges from prose.
- worked table (declared `CXC-IH-*`):

| Bracket as written | Normalized | Candidate? | Verdict |
|--------------------|-----------|-----------|---------|
| `[CXC-IH-5A]`, node exists | `CXC-IH-5A` | yes | clean — it minted |
| `[CXC-IH:5A]` | `CXC-IH-5A` | yes | **inflation failure** — mis-punctuated, mints nothing |
| `[cxc-ih-5a]` | `CXC-IH-5A` | yes | **inflation failure** — lowercase is prose to the parser |
| `[CXC-IH-CONSOLE]`, no such node | `CXC-IH-CONSOLE` | yes | **inflation failure** — typo or unauthored element |
| `[CXC-SUB-DECLARE]` | `CXC-SUB-DECLARE` | no | not a candidate — different family |
| `[see the IH notes]` | `SEE-THE-IH-NOTES` | no | not a candidate — does not match the prefix |

- error-case: a normalized form that is EMPTY after N4 (a bracket of pure punctuation) MUST be discarded
  as a non-candidate, not compared. This is the guard that keeps markdown link syntax and ASCII art out
  of the finding set.
- testability: the whole rule is table-drivable, and the table above is the test's first six rows.
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-DOC-ROOT]

### [DRI-INFLATION-SCOPE] SETTLED by census — the check scans STRUCTURAL POSITIONS only

- decision (settled, not open): the inflation check scans only STRUCTURAL POSITIONS — a bracket that is
  the leading token of a heading, or the leading token of a column-0 list item. Those two positions are
  exactly where D1 and D2 occurred. Brackets in prose, table cells and property values are NOT scanned.
- census (run 2026-08-11 across the corpus; this is the measurement that decided it):

| Quantity | Count |
|----------|-------|
| brackets in STRUCTURAL positions | 2,462 |
| brackets in PROSE positions | 5,617 |
| of those prose brackets, resolving to NO node | 489 |
| of those, matching their OWN document's element family | **215** |

- consequence, and it is decisive: a whole-file scan would fire **215 findings on legitimate prose
  references** — an author naming a planned, hypothetical or cross-discussed element id in a sentence,
  which is a normal and frequent act that this very document performs repeatedly. At ERROR severity that
  is not a noisy check, it is a check that gets switched off, and a switched-off check costs more than
  every miss it would have caught.
- rationale (why the recall loss is affordable): the structural positions are where an author who
  INTENDED a node actually writes one. A mis-punctuated id written inline in running prose is a real miss
  and is knowingly accepted, because 215 false errors is the price of catching it and the corpus has
  already measured what a widened predicate costs — a naive "a `- ` line with a colon" rule ran 96% false
  positive on this same corpus, and the near-miss classifier's own source records deliberately declining
  recall to hold precision.
- requirement: the finding count on a clean corpus is itself an acceptance criterion (DRI-C10), not an
  afterthought. A check whose false-positive rate is not measured has not been validated.
- documents: [DRI-INFLATION]
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-DOC-ROOT]

### [DRI-CONVERSE] The three converse checks — misfiled, dead declaration, namespace leak

- requirement: a declared `elements` set MUST be checked in both directions. Inflation asks "did
  everything the author intended as an element become one?"; the converses ask "is everything that became
  one supposed to be here, and is every declaration doing work?"

| Check | Condition | Severity | Remedy stated in the finding |
|-------|-----------|----------|------------------------------|
| **MISFILED** | a node minted in this file whose id matches NONE of the declared patterns (root exempt) | error | declare the pattern, or move the node to the document that owns its family |
| **DEAD DECLARATION** | a declared pattern with ZERO minted nodes in this file | warning | drop the pattern, or author the elements it promised |
| **NAMESPACE LEAK** | a node in ANOTHER file whose id matches this file's declared pattern, where that other file does not itself declare the pattern | warning | move the node, or declare the pattern in both roots if the family legitimately spans them |

- rationale (why dead declaration is a warning and misfiled is an error): a misfiled node means the graph
  and the declaration disagree about a node that EXISTS — a reader following the declaration will miss
  it. A dead declaration is a promise not yet kept, which is the normal state of a spec being written;
  making it an error would punish authoring order.
- rationale (why leak is a warning): an amendment document extending a spec's family is a legitimate
  shape, and the remedy — declare the pattern in both roots — is one line. An error would force a naming
  migration to satisfy a bookkeeping rule.
- requirement: the leak check requires corpus scope and MUST NOT be attempted from a single-file lint. A
  single-file run MUST report the leak check as NOT RUN rather than as passing, because a check that
  reports green while skipped is the whole defect class in miniature.
- error-case: two files declaring the SAME pattern, both minting nodes under it, is NOT a leak (both
  declared it) but MUST be reported as a SHARED-FAMILY warning naming both files, since a reader of
  either root now has an incomplete list.
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-DOC-ROOT]

---

## 4. The bracketed-key silent-loss class and the control that never exercised it

### [DRI-BRACKET-KEY] A bracketed id in the KEY position must either PARSE or be FLAGGED — never both-nothing

- definition: the shape is a column-0 list item whose body opens with a bracketed id followed by a colon
  and a description. Spec 41's `CXC-IH-EDGES` node — titled "Decompositional edges this realizes" —
  consists of exactly three such lines and nothing else (indented here; a live example, one line shown):

        - [DX-LEAF-OWNS-BEHAVIOR]: the Host owns spawn/lifecycle; the intelligence owns its own work.

- measured: `properties: {}`, `edges: {}`, `edge_total: 0`, zero lint findings of any kind. Cause is two
  anchors agreeing: `_PROP_SIMPLE_RE` requires a lowercase key, so nothing parses; and the coverage
  near-miss classifier's `_KEY_COLON_RE` also requires the line to open with a letter, so the line is not
  even classified as a near-miss. It is prose to both, and prose produces no finding by design.
- requirement: this shape MUST NOT remain in the both-nothing state. Which of the two remedies applies
  was DECIDED by David on 2026-08-11 at [DD-DRI-BRACKET-KEY-VERDICT]: **FLAG, do not mint.** The line
  produces a finding that NAMES it and mints NO edge, and the finding's message MUST carry the repair —
  write `- [ID] **Title**` to mint a NODE, or move the line under `## Relationships` as
  `- [SRC] VERB [TARGET]` to mint a TYPED edge. The current outcome — a node that exists to declare three
  edges declaring none, silently — was unacceptable under either branch and is now closed under this one.
- requirement: the class MUST be registered in the silent-loss register with a name, a measured count,
  and a control case. The register at
  `docs/improvement_suggestions/coordination/silent-loss-classes.md` is the numbering authority and is
  READ, not restated here; at its v5 state the highest assigned number is SL-18, so this class takes the
  next free number at merge time rather than one reserved by this document.
- measured count for the register: 3 occurrences, all in `41-intelligence-host-spawner.lts` — which is
  also one of the five files being repaired ([DRI-REPAIR]), so both edits land in one pass on one file.
- criticality: high
- see-also: [DD-DRI-BRACKET-KEY-VERDICT], [DRI-CONTROL-CASES]
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-DOC-ROOT]

### [DRI-CONTROL-SHAPE] A control that does not exercise a LIVE shape certifies a narrower class than its name claims

- principle (this is the generalizable result, and it outlives the specific defect): a control case named
  for a CLASS but authored around one INSTANCE certifies only that instance. The name then travels — into
  a passing selfcheck, into a status table, into an agent's summary — carrying an assurance the control
  never produced.
- evidence: `CASE-SL1-SYMBOL` is named "a symbol-prefixed key does not parse, and IS flagged". Its body
  authors an emoji-prefixed key. The class it NAMES covers every non-`[a-z]` opening character in the key
  position; the shape found in live corpora — an opening `[` — is one of those, and the control does not
  contain it. The selfcheck was green while the corpus lost three edges.
- requirement: every silent-loss class MUST have its control cross-checked against a CORPUS SHAPE CENSUS
  — the actual line shapes that occur in real documents — and not only against shapes invented while
  writing the control. Where the census produces a shape the control does not contain, the control MUST
  be extended or the class MUST be renamed to what it actually covers.
- requirement: where no live shape backs a class, that class MUST be labelled THEORETICAL in the register
  (two classes already are), so the difference between "checked against reality" and "checked against
  imagination" stays visible in the one place people read.
- rationale: the corpus already contains the proof that a positive control can be satisfied by the wrong
  thing — a detector that fired correctly fourteen times and named the wrong file every time. The lesson
  compounds here: a control's SHAPE COVERAGE is as load-bearing as its assertion, and neither is implied
  by the other.
- consequence for reviewers: "the selfcheck is green" is not an answer to "is this class handled". The
  answerable question is "which live shapes does the control contain", and it must be answerable by
  reading the control.
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-DOC-ROOT]

### [DRI-CONTROL-CASES] The control cases this spec requires, authored in the LIVE shape

- requirement: the selfcheck control file MUST gain the cases below. Each MUST be authored in the shape
  found in a live corpus, and each MUST assert BOTH what was minted and what was flagged — a case
  asserting only one half is how a both-nothing outcome passes.

| Case | Shape authored in the control | Asserts |
|------|-------------------------------|---------|
| bracketed key, key position | a column-0 list item opening `- [ID]: description` inside a node body | the ruled verdict at [DD-DRI-BRACKET-KEY-VERDICT] — a finding NAMES the line, NO edge is minted, and the message contains both repairs (`- [ID] **Title**`, and the `## Relationships` triple) |
| missing frontmatter | a file opening directly with an h1, no `---` block | an error naming the rule and the exemplar |
| frontmatter without `audience` | a complete block with `audience` removed | an error naming the rule and the exemplar |
| frontmatter without `elements` | a complete block with `elements` removed | an error naming the rule and the exemplar |
| over-broad declaration | a file whose elements are `PFX-SUB-*` declaring `PFX-*` | an error naming the narrowest covering pattern ([DRI-ELEMENTS-NARROW]) |
| shared declaration | two files declaring the same pattern | a finding naming both |
| **derived declaration** | a file with an authored id that never minted, plus a declaration built only from its minted nodes | the derived declaration does NOT cover the known id — the assertion that catches a fitted control ([DRI-ELEMENTS-INTENT]) |
| bracketless h1 | `# Title` with no id, plus a column-0 `[TAG:ID]` line below it | an error naming the h1 rule AND naming the bare line as the probable intended root |
| non-SPEC tags | an h1 bearing `[LEDGER:ID]` and one bearing `[REGISTRY:ID]` | both clean — the rule is over all registered tags, not `SPEC` alone |
| untagged h1 id | `# Title [ID]` with no doctype | a finding — an untagged root is invisible to `find(doctype=…)` |
| two h1 ids | two `h1` lines each carrying an id | an error naming both lines; no precedence resolution |
| inflation, mis-punctuated | a declared `elements` prefix plus a `[PFX:5A]`-shaped bracket in a STRUCTURAL position | an inflation failure naming the raw and normalized forms |
| inflation, lowercase | the same with a lowercase bracket | an inflation failure |
| inflation, non-candidate | a bracket of pure punctuation; a bracket from a different family; **and a matching bracket in PROSE** | NO finding on any of the three — the precision half, including the 215-case exclusion ([DRI-INFLATION-SCOPE]) |
| misfiled / dead / leak | a node outside every declared pattern; a pattern with no nodes; a node in a second file under this file's pattern | one finding each, at the severities in [DRI-CONVERSE] |

- requirement: the non-candidate row is not optional, and its prose sub-case is the one that guards
  [DRI-INFLATION-SCOPE]'s ruling. A check whose control contains only positives measures recall and says
  nothing about the false-positive rate, and the false-positive rate is what decides whether a check
  survives contact with a 72-file corpus.
- see-also: [DRI-CONTROL-SHAPE]
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-DOC-ROOT]

---

## 5. The constraint that forecloses the tempting migration

### [DRI-ID-GRAMMAR] Element ids are CORRECT as authored and MUST NOT be re-namespaced — the colon is taken

- fact (checked in the grammar, 2026-08-11): node ids match `^[A-Z][A-Z0-9_\-]*$`. Uppercase, digits,
  underscore and HYPHEN are permitted. There is no colon, no dot, and an id may not begin with a digit.
- fact: the colon is ALREADY the doctype separator. The parser's own contract reads
  `# Title [DOCTYPE:ID] -> document_tag=DOCTYPE`, which is why `[SPEC:CXC-MODULES]` yields
  `document_tag: "SPEC"`. That is also exactly what makes [DRI-ROOT-BY-DOCTYPE]'s table work.
- requirement (a REJECTED design, recorded so a future reader does not re-derive it): element ids MUST
  NOT be re-namespaced into a `[FAMILY:ELEMENT]` form such as `[CXC-IH:5A]`. Under the existing grammar
  that parses as doctype `CXC-IH` plus id `5A`; `5A` begins with a digit and fails the id grammar; so the
  line mints NOTHING. The migration that looks like it would clarify element namespacing would
  manufacture, corpus-wide and by construction, the exact silent-loss failure this spec exists to end.
- key-insight: `CXC-IH-HOST` already reads as CXC / IH / HOST. The hyphen is the only separator ids have,
  and it is sufficient — a prefix pattern over a hyphen-structured id is exactly the trailing-glob test
  [DRI-ELEMENTS-DECL] needs. Nothing about the current ids is deficient; what was missing was a way to
  DECLARE the family, which is what the `elements` property adds without touching a single id.
- consequence: any proposal to change element id shape must first change the id grammar and the doctype
  separator, which are load-bearing for every document in every corpus. That cost is not in scope here
  and the benefit is nil, so this is closed, not open.
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-DOC-ROOT]

---

## 6. The decision point — CLOSED, ruled by David 2026-08-11

### [DD-DRI-BRACKET-KEY-VERDICT] Does `- [ID]: description` PARSE into an edge, or get FLAGGED for re-authoring?

- decision: CLOSED — RULED by David 2026-08-11: **FLAG. Do not mint.** A column-0 list item of the shape
  `- [ID]: description` is reported as a NAMED FINDING and mints NO edge. The finding's message MUST
  carry the repair, because the repair is trivial and the author must be handed what they meant
  EXPLICITLY rather than have it guessed for them: write `- [ID] **Title**` to mint a NODE, or move the
  line under `## Relationships` as `- [SRC] VERB [TARGET]` to mint a TYPED edge. Both repairs are
  well-formed under today's grammar and neither requires a parser change.
- context: the shape's author intent is not in doubt — spec 41's node is literally titled
  "Decompositional edges this realizes" and its three lines name three targets. The question is whether a
  parser may act on an intent whose TYPE is nowhere on the line.
- rationale: the edge TYPE is not in the syntax — it lives in the SECTION TITLE. Minting an untyped edge
  from this line therefore means inventing a semantic the author never wrote, and an invented edge is a
  SUBSTITUTION. This corpus has already established that a substitution is strictly worse than a LOSS: a
  missing edge is DETECTABLE — by the inflation check, the three converse checks and the line accounting
  specified in this very document — and a plausible wrong one is not. SL-18 is the recorded instance:
  `zb.ds.GOVERNS` silently became `GOVERNS` and the result looked well-formed, which is exactly why
  nothing caught it. The corpus has ALREADY paid for a widened matcher fabricating an edge to a target
  named `"-55]"`, emitted from a verb matched as a substring, with line accounting blind to it because
  the line was CONSUMED.
- rationale: CONSISTENCY ACROSS THE CLASS, and this is the decisive new evidence. David ruled
  identically, hours earlier on 2026-08-11, on the parenthetical / em-dash qualifier drops
  (`- key (qualifier): value`) — for the same reason and with the same repair shape: FLAG the line, do
  not guess at the key, and repair by moving the qualifier into the VALUE. That class is live in this
  very document, at 28 `near_miss_qualified_key_paren` findings across specs 58 and 59 alone. Ruling MINT
  here would resolve two ambiguous-key situations in OPPOSITE directions and make the format incoherent
  with itself. Consistency across the class is part of the ruling, not a decoration on it.
- rationale: the marginal cost of the flag branch is now approximately zero — three lines in one file,
  re-authored as either `- realizes: [DX-LEAF-OWNS-BEHAVIOR]` or a Relationships triple, and that file is
  `41-intelligence-host-spawner.lts`, which is already being edited for [DRI-REPAIR].
- alternatives: (a) PARSE AS AN EDGE — genuinely defensible, and its strongest form is not the weak
  `RELATED_TO` default: mint the edge with an explicit UNTYPED marker, so the graph records "the author
  declared a relationship here and did not type it" rather than either dropping it or guessing. That
  preserves the knowledge, is honest about what is known, and would recover three edges without any
  authoring change — and it generalizes to every future document that uses the shape. Its cost is a new
  edge state that every consumer must now handle, and the format currently has no untyped edge. (b)
  PARSE AS A PROPERTY whose key is the bracketed id — cheapest to implement and it stops the loss, but it
  produces a property key that is not a property key by the grammar, and a downstream consumer filtering
  on lowercase keys drops it again silently. (c) DO NOTHING and rely on the inflation check to catch it —
  insufficient: the targets are ids from another family (`DX-*`), so a `CXC-IH-*` declaration would not
  make them candidates, and the loss would stay invisible.
- consequence: three downstream statements in this document lose their disjunction and become decided
  behaviour. [DRI-CONTROL-CASES]' bracketed-key row asserts that a finding NAMES the line and that NO
  edge is minted, rather than "whichever branch ratifies". [DRI-BOUNDARIES] row 24 states FLAG as the
  required behaviour. DRI-C15 asserts the finding exists rather than asserting a disjunction. No model
  change is required, and no consumer that enumerates edge types gains a case.
- consequence: had (a) been ratified instead, an untyped-edge state would have entered the model, the
  control case at [DRI-CONTROL-CASES] would assert the edge exists rather than that a finding names the
  line, and every consumer that enumerates edge types would gain a case. That is a MODEL change and it
  belongs to the architect, not to a linter fix — which is exactly why this was a decision node and not
  a default.
- change: "RULED — `- [ID]: description` is FLAGGED as a named finding and mints NO edge; the finding
  carries its own repair. The rejected branch is minting an explicitly UNTYPED edge. Ruled by David
  2026-08-11, consistently with the same day's ruling on parenthetical qualifier drops."
- context_for_change:
    prior_state: "The node stood at status `proposed` — FLAG, do not parse — with branch (a), an
      explicitly UNTYPED edge, recorded as genuinely defensible and unresolved. The corpus meanwhile
      carried three authored edges in `41-intelligence-host-spawner.lts` that reached no parser branch,
      produced `edges: {}`, `edge_total: 0`, and zero lint findings of any kind."
    reasoning: "The edge TYPE is not in the syntax; it is in the section title. Minting from this line
      invents a semantic the author never wrote, and an invented edge is a SUBSTITUTION rather than a
      loss. A missing edge is detectable by the checks this spec already specifies; a plausible wrong one
      is not, and it is not detectable by anything, which is why SL-18 (`zb.ds.GOVERNS` silently becoming
      `GOVERNS`) survived a green selfcheck. The corpus has already paid once for a widened matcher
      fabricating an edge to the target `-55]`. Decisively, the same ruling was made hours earlier on
      the parenthetical qualifier drops, for the same reason and with the same repair shape: two
      ambiguous-key situations resolved in opposite directions would make the format incoherent with
      itself, so consistency across the class is part of the ruling."
    rejected_alternative: "Mint an explicitly UNTYPED edge. At full strength this is the best argument
      available for the other side: it recovers [CXC-IH-EDGES]' three real, genuinely-intended lost edges
      with no authoring change at all; it is HONEST about what is known, recording 'the author declared a
      relationship here and did not type it' rather than either dropping the knowledge or guessing a
      verb; and it GENERALISES to every future document that reaches for the shape, so the fix scales
      while the flag branch's repair cost scales with the population. It is not a weak `RELATED_TO`
      default and it should not be dismissed as one."
    counter_argument: "An untyped edge is not a fabrication — it asserts strictly less than the author
      wrote, never more, so it cannot be the SUBSTITUTION the ruling objects to. Refusing it therefore
      chooses a certain, measured loss of three real edges over a marker that is honest by construction."
    counter_response: "Conceded that an untyped edge does not fabricate a VERB. What it fabricates is a
      STATE: the model has no untyped edge, so minting one introduces an edge state the model does not
      have. That is a MODEL change riding in on a parser convenience, and it arrives without any of the
      consumer work a model change owes — every enumeration of edge types, every renderer, every
      traversal that switches on type gains an unhandled case, silently, on the first document that uses
      the shape. The three real edges are recoverable for the cost of three re-authored lines in a file
      that is already being edited for [DRI-REPAIR]. The objection is to inventing the state HERE, as a
      side effect, rather than to the state existing."
    invalidating_assumption: "That the untyped-edge state stays ABSENT from the model. If FactLattice
      ever gains a first-class untyped or `mentions`-style edge as a RATIFIED concept — declared in the
      model, handled by every consumer, and arrived at on its own merits rather than as a parser
      convenience — then this decision reopens and (a) becomes free, because the cost this ruling
      declines to pay will already have been paid. Secondarily invalidated by population: this shape is 3
      occurrences in one file of 72, and if a census of other corpora finds it is a common authoring
      idiom, the flag branch's migration cost scales with that population while (a)'s fabrication risk
      does not."
    implication: "[DRI-CONTROL-CASES]' bracketed-key row, [DRI-BOUNDARIES] row 24 and DRI-C15 all lose
      their disjunction and state the FLAG outcome. [DRI-BRACKET-KEY]'s requirement is no longer
      conditional. The three lines in `41-intelligence-host-spawner.lts` are re-authored in the same pass
      as [DRI-REPAIR]. The silent-loss class still takes its register number at merge time; the ruling
      settles the class's REMEDY, not its number."
- status: decided
- invalidating_assumption: "That the untyped-edge state stays ABSENT from the model. If FactLattice ever
  gains a first-class untyped or `mentions`-style edge as a RATIFIED concept — declared in the model,
  handled by every consumer, and arrived at on its own merits rather than as a parser convenience — then
  this decision reopens and (a) becomes free, because the cost this ruling declines to pay will already
  have been paid. Secondarily invalidated by population: this shape is 3 occurrences in one file of 72,
  and if a census of other corpora finds it is a common authoring idiom, the flag branch's migration cost
  scales with that population while (a)'s fabrication risk does not."
- documents: [DRI-BRACKET-KEY]
- part-of: [SPEC:LTS-DOC-ROOT]

